{
  "summary": "The prior ideation rounds have thoroughly mapped the decomposition space and reached strong consensus on the three-tier strategy, but this fourth cycle should focus on what's been consistently underspecified: the concrete file-level extraction plan for Tier 1 and the exact interface contract for Tier 2. Tier 1 is genuinely zero-risk and can be executed mechanically today: lines 85-490 (7 pure functions: _last_modified_key, handle_cli_commands, _handle_config_command, _handle_context_command, _feature_markdown, _escape_markup, _format_json_text, _build_feature_detail_widgets) move to pipeline/tui_helpers.py with a single `from tui_helpers import *` in tui.py preserving all call sites. This is a 400-line reduction with zero behavioral change, testable with standard pytest, and should be committed independently. For Tier 2, the prior rounds correctly identify the @work constraint but haven't acknowledged a simpler middle ground: rather than a full event-bus/PipelineController redesign, the ~25 synchronous action_* methods (action_move, action_approve, action_reject, action_restore, action_new_feature, etc.) can be split into a decision/validation layer extracted as pure functions and a thin dispatch layer remaining on PipelineApp. For example, action_approve's logic of 'determine next stage, validate preconditions, move file' is pure data transformation that doesn't need @work or self access beyond reading feature state — extract that logic, keep only the self.push_screen/self.notify calls on PipelineApp. This avoids the God Object anti-pattern flagged in Round 6 because extracted functions receive narrow typed arguments (feature data dict, current stage string) rather than the full PipelineApp instance. The _check_for_changes() polling problem should remain a separate workstream as all prior rounds agree, but the simplest immediate fix is adding an mtime cache dict and short-circuiting when no file's mtime has changed — no external dependency needed, reduces the hot path from O(n) file reads to O(n) stat calls with early exit."
}
